WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.600
After months of eager anticipation, Captain
Marvel is finally upon us.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.540
And so far, both critics and fans think it's
pretty darn okay.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:10.140
But that doesn't mean it's all perfect.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.160
Here's a look at the four best and four worst
things about Captain Marvel.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:19.339
The film is called Captain Marvel, which obviously
means there was very little chance of it working

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.520
without the right talent in the title role.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:26.440
Brie Larson had to not only be the first woman
to be the sole lead in a Marvel Studios film,

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.659
but she also had to play a character who's
so beloved by comics fans that the collective

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.120
fandom has their own name : the Carol Corps.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.140
And she had to do it all without the benefit
of having been introduced as a supporting

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.020
character first.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.520
Even with all that on her shoulders, Larson
knocked it out of the park.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:47.640
Her Carol Danvers is capable, hilarious, empathetic,
strong, vulnerable, and intimidating all at

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.900
once, an acting challenge made all the more
difficult by the script's demand that she

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.420
not even truly know who she is for most of
the film.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.910
Larson was already an Oscar-winning star when
she was cast in this role, but now she's headed

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:01.380
for superstardom because she nailed this part.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.140
The films of Marvel Studios have taken plenty
of flack in the past for the way they look

00:01:06.159 --> 00:01:10.219
and feel, as fans and critics have noticed
a similar generic style that seems to cross

00:01:10.219 --> 00:01:13.210
between films regardless of who is directing
it.

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:17.429
Sure, there are some outliers like Guardians
of the Galaxy and Thor: Ragnarok, but many

00:01:17.429 --> 00:01:22.340
Marvel movies share a certain murky, dull
cinematography, as well as some standard fight

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.860
choreography and unfortunately, Captain Marvel
is no exception.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:30.960
This is especially evident during the early
fight scene on Torfa, where the Kree StarForce

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.320
is ambushed by Skrulls.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:36.759
Even action sequences like the train chase
on Earth feel paint-by-numbers.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.120
It's just too formulaic to be interesting
anymore.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:43.560
On the other hand, Captain Marvel definitely
shone when it broke out of the standard Marvel

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:44.060
box.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.840
Case in point: the memory probe sequence,
which features alien Skrulls flipping back

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:53.920
and forth through Carol's memories like DJs
scratching a record, all while providing snarky

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:54.420
commentary.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.040
It was so odd and entertaining that you barely
noticed the fact it also was serving up massive

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.040
amounts of key exposition about both Carol
Danvers and the film's plot.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:04.440
Now that's how it's done!

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:09.140
With so much information flying at viewers,
though, it can be easy to get confused.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.640
So it doesn't help when the film is genuinely
confusing, like when it has a hard time explaining

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:14.700
its own timeline.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:19.560
The film starts with an amnesiac Carol living
on the Kree homeworld of Hala, trying to land

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.300
her first mission with the elite StarForce
commando squad.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:27.080
Later on, though, we discover it's actually
been six years since she was taken from Earth.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:31.590
Since she seems a bit like a rookie, it's
jarring, as it forces you to ask: just what

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:33.680
has she been doing for the last six years?

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.320
Why hasn't she searched for clues to her past
before?

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.500
It's a minor detail, but it pulls you out
of the movie right when you need to be pulled

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.000
in.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:45.920
As great as Brie Larson is in the title role
of the film, Samuel L. Jackson has been stealing

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.160
scenes for decades, and Captain Marvel is
no exception.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:53.720
Jackson seems to be having a blast as a much
younger, more carefree Nick Fury than we're

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:54.620
used to seeing.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:59.760
Less jaded and more open, this Fury trades
one-liners as easily as he bonds with the

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.860
film's other scene-stealer, Goose the cat.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.140
This is a Fury we'd love to see more of.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:08.840
"Aren’t you cute, what’s your name?”

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.000
“Fury!"

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:14.320
Throughout the film, Danvers is constantly
picked on by her commanding officer, Jude

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:15.320
Law's Yon-Rogg.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.660
He and the other Kree repeat over and over
again that she needs to control and suppress

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.640
her true self in order to become what they
want her to become.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:23.700
"Control it!"

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:29.320
It's a big metaphor for how society forces
people to conform to other people's ideals.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:34.280
But while the payoff is ultimately satisfying,
the setup doesn't feel organic; Law often

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:38.540
feels like he's simply reading the movie's
mission statement rather than saying things

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.230
a person would actually say.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:43.790
"it's causing you doubt, and doubt makes you
vulnerable."

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:48.550
Perhaps it's asking too much of a superhero
movie to be subtle, but a little bit of nuance

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:49.540
would have gone a long way.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:54.160
"You've come a long way, but you're not as
a strong as you think!"

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:58.550
On the other hand, like we said: the payoff
is great once we get there.

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:02.760
In the film's climax, Danvers faces the Kree
leader known as the Supreme Intelligence,

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:07.060
who reveals that the Kree have been suppressing
her powers in an attempt to control her.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.370
All their talk of self-control was essentially
the Kree trying to gaslight her into believing

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:14.260
she wasn't as strong as she really is.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:19.160
Instead, though, Danvers breaks free from
their inhibitors and embraces her full humanity,

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:20.480
unlocking her true power.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.530
Instead of being controlled, prim and proper,
she summons all the self-confidence, anger,

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.260
and righteousness that have been bottled up..

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:29.530
"I'm kinda done with you telling me what I
can't do."

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.240
It's the culmination of everything the film
wants to say about Carol Danvers and suppressed

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:35.540
people in general.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:38.400
It's cheer-worthy, and it may even bring a
tear to your eye.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:42.900
There is one minor, sour note in the otherwise
triumphant ending, though.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:47.280
When Danvers faces off against her former
StarForce teammates, it should be powerful.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:49.320
But it's not, because the stakes are unclear.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:53.280
Had she been training with these people for
six years, or was she just added to the team

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:54.320
for that one mission?

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:55.220
Are they friends?

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:00.160
Both side have reason to feel betrayed, yet
other than a couple of throwaway lines, we

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.360
never really see any emotion from this faceoff.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:05.380
And we also have no idea how powerful any
of them are.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.100
They don't really put a dent in Danvers, but
they also manage to survive her attacks, which

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.520
is more than the other Kree soldiers can say.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.960
Without knowing how powerful her enemies are,
it's impossible to feel like Danvers is ever

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:20.400
in any real danger, meaning that the physical
stakes are as low as the emotional ones.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:25.300
As a result, the final showdown doesn't land
with the emotional impact a film like this

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:25.800
deserves.

